WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Welcome back to Tech Unfiltered. I'm your host Maya Chen, and today we have a special guest - Alex Rivera, founder of DataMesh, which just raised their Series B at a $500 million valuation. Alex, welcome to the show.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Thanks for having me, Maya. Big fan of the podcast.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:35.000
So let's jump right in. DataMesh has been called the "anti-data-warehouse." For listeners who aren't familiar, can you explain what that means?

00:00:35.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Sure. Traditional data warehouses force you to move all your data to one central location. That made sense 20 years ago. But now companies have data in dozens of systems - Salesforce, Snowflake, MongoDB, you name it. Moving it all is expensive, slow, and creates compliance nightmares. We flip that model. Your data stays where it is, and we create a virtual layer that lets you query across all of it.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:20.000
That sounds like data virtualization, which has been around for a while. What's different about your approach?

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Great question. Traditional virtualization is slow because it queries source systems in real-time. We built a smart caching layer that learns your query patterns. It pre-fetches data you're likely to need. Our benchmarks show we're within 10% of native warehouse performance, but without the ETL headaches.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:05.000
That's impressive. Let's talk about the founding story. You were at Google before this, right?

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Yeah, I spent six years on the BigQuery team. I loved the technical challenges, but I kept seeing the same pattern with enterprise customers. They'd spend months migrating data, build pipelines, then realize they forgot a crucial data source. Start over. It was Groundhog Day. I knew there had to be a better way.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:50.000
When did you decide to actually start the company?

00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Honestly, it was a conversation with a customer. They told me they had spent $2 million on data infrastructure and still couldn't answer basic questions about their business. That night I went home and started sketching out what became DataMesh.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:30.000
And now you've raised over $80 million total. What does the fundraising process look like from the inside?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:04:00.000
It's a rollercoaster. For the Series A, we got 40 rejections before Sequoia said yes. This time was different - we had revenue and happy customers. But the pressure is higher too. Investors expect you to 10x in three years. You're constantly balancing growth against sustainability.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:15.000
What advice would you give to founders who are just starting out?

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Three things. First, talk to customers before you write any code. I spent three months just doing interviews. Second, find a co-founder who complements you - my CTO handles the hard engineering while I focus on product and go-to-market. Third, and this is the hardest one, be patient. We were "overnight success" that took five years.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Wise words. Alex Rivera, thanks so much for joining us today. DataMesh - we'll put links in the show notes.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Thanks Maya. This was fun.
